Graphene/MOF-based composite materials in three-dimensional (3D) architectures have received tremendous attention in recent years due to their superior physiochemical properties and more application capacities than the two-dimensional (2D) graphenebased materials.[1] The 3D graphene/MOF-based architectures with adjustable porous skeleton and excellent mechanical strength not only preserve the intrinsic properties of their constituents, but prevent the aggregation and restacking of graphene nanosheets as well as sheet imperfections, thereby enabling their performance upgrading in various domains.[2] Another new feature exploited for 3D graphene/MOF-based materials is the superwettability with extreme repulsive effects on oil or water,[3] which show great potential in oil-spill cleaning by simple and efficient absorption. Numerous graphene-based absorbing materials such as supported or self-supporting graphene foam/sponges have been developed since 2012.[4] However, most of them are often constructed from the 2D nascent graphene nanosheets, which probably cause nanosheet restacking and slow the molecular diffusion rate, limiting the absorption capability and efficiency. It has been found that kneading the graphene nanosheets will significantly increase the hydrophobicity and even up to superhydrophobicity (water contact angle ${>}150^{\circ}.$ ).[5] It is anticipated that further assembly of the crumpled graphene nanosheets into rough-surfaced microspheres can obtain both superhydrophobicity and superoleophilicity (oil contact angle ${<}10^{\circ})$ ,[6] which is crucial for efficient oil absorption in the presence of water. However, so far there is no report of wrinkled graphene-based microspheres being used as absorbing materials for oil-water separation.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an emerging porous crystalline material with periodic network structure, constructed by self-assembly of metal units and organic linkers.[7] Credited with their versatile architectures and customizable chemical functionalities, MOFs have been extensively explored in variety of fields.[8] Apart from the substantial characteristics of high porosity, ordered pores with exceptional high internal surface area, a number of MOFs based on fluorine-containing linkers and/or long-chain alkyl groups are highly hydrophobic,[9] which inhibit the penetration of water molecules into pores. Some advancement on MOF adsorbents for oil-water separation applications has been achieved in recent years.[9c,10] In view of this, a concept of powerful synergy for enhanced oil-water separation would be the hybridization of MOFs with the wrinkled graphene-based microspheres to form the urchin-like hierarchically porous structure. Note that this mechanism is remarkably different from the reported 3D MOF/graphene composite materials that are either the grafting of MOFs into the nascent GO nanosheets or coating the graphene (or its derivatives) onto the MOFs forming the core–shell architectures.[11] Taking into consideration the new collective physiochemical properties, decoration of the wrinkled graphenebased microspheres with adaptable MOFs is expected to realize higher selectivity and stronger absorption capacity as well as recyclable stability for removal of oils from water. Herein we present the fabrication of the sphere-shaped MOF@rGO composites consisting of wrinkled reduced GO (rGO) and intercalated MOF nanoparticles, aiming at providing alternative candidates for robust superhydrophobic/superoleophilic materials applied in efficient oil-water separation or other antiwater applications.
To achieve this goal, in this study we selected the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs),[12] a subfamily of MOFs, such as ZIF-8 [13] to decorate the wrinkled rGO microspheres because of its easy synthesis, abundant microporosity as well as exceptional chemical stability and flexibility. The wrinkled 3D microspherical MOF@rGO composites could be facilely formed by a high-temperature reduction self-assembly strategy which contains only two steps (as shown in Scheme 1). Firstly, the ZIF8 nanoparticles were in situ grown into the rGO nanosheets dispersed in water at the room temperature, forming the building elements of $Z1F{-}8@\mathsf{G O}$ nanosheets. After addition of a certain amount of reducing agent (L-ascorbic acid), the $Z1F{-}8@\mathsf{G O}$ suspension was atomized as microdroplets by an ultrasonicspray nozzle, and then sprayed downward into a continuously stirred methylsilicone oil $(50m>a\cdot s)$ with a constant high temperature of $165^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The ZIF-8@GO nanosheets inside were fast wrinkled and assembled into the porous ZIF- $8@{\mathsf{r G O}}$ microspheres benefiting from rapid evaporation of water and effect of reducing agent.
The TEM and SEM images (Figure 1a and Fig. S1a) show the urchin-shaped microspherical structure of ZIF- $8@\mathsf{r G O}$ with a diameter of about $2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . From the high-resolution images (Figure 1b and Fig. S1b), the microsphere is composed of curly rGO nanosheets intercalated with abundant ZIF-8 nanoparticles. Importantly, the ZIF-8 nanoparticles are highly-dispersed in the $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}8@{\mathsf{r}}{\mathsf{G O}}$ microsphere, which can be verified from the uniform distribution of corresponding elements detected by the Energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDXS) (Figure 1d-h). However, we found that although reduction of the GO can be proved from the XRD patterns and FTIR spectra, no obvious characteristic peaks related to the ZIF-8 were detected in ZIF-8@rGO microsphere (Fig. S2,S3), which was due to the rigorous wrapping of rGO nanosheets and the poor crystallinity of tiny ZIF-8 nanoparicles (with a size of $5\ \mathrm{to}\ 10\ \mathrm{nm})$ in microsphere. The particle sizes of the ZIF-8 in the microsphere range from 5 to 10 nm, which is much less than the synthesized ZIF-8 $(150\sim350\ \mathrm{nm})$ (Fig. S4a,b) in solution without GO. This is due to the confinement effect inside the GO interlayers inhibiting the ZIF-8 growth (Fig. S5).[14] The implementation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles between the rGO layers provides additional mesoporosity, and produces more hierarchically rough structure on the wrinkled microsphere surface, considerably affecting the wetting properties of the resultant ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathfrak{r}\mathsf{G O}$ composite. As shown in Figure 1i, the Z $\mathsf{I F}{-}8@\mathsf{r G O}$ exhibits a high water contact angle of $158^{\circ}$ and ultrafast change of ethanol contact angle from $2^{\circ}$ to $0^{\circ}$ in a very short time of 0.15 s, demonstrating an excellent superhydrophobic along with superoleophilic behavior. Accordingly, the ZIF-8@rGO microsphere was tested for the separation of organic solvents and oils from water, and showed stronger absorption capacity (Fig. S6) compared to the individual ZIF-8 and rGO microsphere, in spite of the strong hydrophobicity of the latter two (Fig. S4c,d, S7). In addition, the composite material of wrinkled ZIF-8@rGO microsphere has a higher thermal stability than the ZIF-8 and rGO microsphere (Fig. S8). These features and synergic merits recommend the wrinkled microspherical MOF@rGO composites for constructing advanced absorbing materials for oil-containing wastewater treatment.
Based on the above results, we further fabricated ZIF8@rGO@Sponge composite as the absorbing materials by a simple dipping-coating method (Figure 2a). A piece of commercial polyurethane (PU) sponge was used as the support due to its low cost, macroporous nature and high absorption to both water and oil (Fig. S9). The PU sponge was first immersed into a polydimethylsiloxane siloxane (PDMS) pre-crosslinked solution mixed with a certain amount of ZIF-8@rGO powders for vacuum treatment about 10 min, and then dried in an oven at $80^{\circ}$ C for several hours to completely cure the PDMS chains. In this way, the ZIF-8@rGO microspheres were tightly adhered into the both internal and external skeletons of the porous sponge, and the loading could be regulated by changing the additive amount of ZIF-8@rGO microspheres. The loading here is defined as the mass of ZIF-8@rGO per cubic centimeter sponge after immersion. Figure 2b-g shows the micro-morphologies of the original PU sponge and the $Z1F{-}8@\mathsf{r G O}@$ Sponge with different loadings. We can clearly see that the ZIF-8@rGO microspheres distributed uniformly onto the interlaced branches of the sponge, and their surfaces are not covered by the coated PDMS layer.
Loading with $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}8@\mathsf{r}{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{O}}$ greatly changed the properties of the PU sponge. As shown in Figure 3a, the pure PU sponge (white color) sank to the beakers bottom while a ZIF8@rGO@Sponge (black color) always floated on the water surface. Moreover, even if immersed in water by an external force, the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge could instantaneously float up after the force was released, and no water uptake was found. This demonstrates the PU sponge became superhydrophobicity with extreme repulsion to water. The silver mirror-like appearance of the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge underwater (the inset in Figure 3a) is attributed to a uniform air layer trapped between the water and hydrophobic surfaces. Figure 3b shows a water droplet in spherical shape on the surface of the ZIF8@rGO@Sponge. In contrast, when silicone oil was dropped, it was immediately absorbed by the sponge, as indicated by the red circle marked area. The insets in Figure $36$ are optical images of the water and oil droplet profile on the surface of ZIF8@rGO@Sponge with a contact angle of $171^{\circ}$ and $0^{\circ}$ . The ZIF$8@{\mathsf{r G O@}}$ Sponge also kept high contact angle when subjected to contact the droplets of saline water, acid water and alkaline water (Fig. S10). The formed no-wetting and superoleophilic surfaces can be attributed to the combination of the superhydrophobic property of the $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}8@\mathsf{r}{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{O}}$ microspheres, and the micro/nano-hierarchically rough structure of the ZIF$8@{\mathsf{r G O}}$ loaded sponge skeletons. To evaluate the absorption selectivity, we used the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge to remove the floated silicone oil and the chloroform (dyed with Oil Red) underwater from water (Fig. S10).[16] As shown in Figure 3c-d, when a piece of ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge was forced into contact with the silicone oil layer or the chloroform in water, it completely absorbed them within several seconds depending on the amount, resulting in the transparent region (clean water) without contaminations. Subsequently, the oil or organic solvent was entirely removed by taking the sponge out, suggesting a simple and a useful route for cleaning oil spillage.
The absorption of ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge for various oil and organic solvents were evaluated, and the absorption capacity was used to measure how much oils or organic solvents were captured (please see the measurement approach in supporting information). First, we investigated the effect of $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}8@{\mathsf{r}}{\mathsf{G O}}$ loading by evaluating the chloroform and water adsorption quantity of $Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{r G O}@\mathsf{s}$ Sponge. As shown in Fig. S12, there is scarcely any water adsorption at whatever ZIF-8@rGO loading. The best absorption capacity for chloroform is at the loading of $0.04~{\mathfrak{g}}/{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}$ , but decreases slightly with the further increase of loading, which could be due to the clogging of sponge pores by the excessive amounts of $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}8@{\mathsf{r}}{\mathsf{G O}}$ microspheres (Figure 2ef). Moreover, the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge with the optimal loading $(0.04~\mathrm{\mathfrak{g/cm}}^{3})$ shows the excellent absorption capacities for organic solvents and oils achieving $1400{\sim}2900~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , depending on the oil or solvents (Figure 4a). The absorbed oils and organic solvents could be easily collected by squeezing. Simultaneously, when subjected to the oil/water mixture, the ZIF8@rGO@Sponge can selectively block the water and allowed oil to pass by, with an ultrahigh separation efficiency (above $98\%$ ) estimated by a reported approach.[17] It should be noted that in despite of the higher absorption capacity in PDMS@Sponge and $r G O@$ Sponge (Fig. S13-S15), the separation efficiencies were only about $50\text{\textperthousand}$ (Fig. S16) far below the ZIF8@rGO@Sponge. This also verified the powerful synergy of ZIF-8 and wrinkled graphene-based microspheres within the sponge. As shown in Figure 4c, the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge keeps its high absorption capacity of oils after 100 cycles, indicating good recyclability. More importantly, the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge sustains a high mechanical strength and superhydrophobicity after the repeated squeezing, because the microspheres firmly stayed in the interlaced branches of sponge (Fig. S17), no obvious falling off occurred. Despite the different evaluation conditions, at least the absorption performance and stability of the ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge in this study are competitive and even superior to other reported absorbing materials (please see a detailed comparison in Table S1).[10c,14,15] This demonstrates a perspective for practical applications of ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge in oil/chemical cleanup applications.
In addition, our approach applied to the large-sized ZIF-8 (hundreds of nanometers) decorated graphene-based wrinkled microspheres (Fig. S18). Other ZIFs such as ZIF-7, ZIF-11 and ZIF-67 (Fig. S19a-c)[12] could be mingled with the GO nanosheets to form the wrinkled microspherical $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{7@r G O}}$ , ZIF-11@rGO and ZIF-67@rGO composites (Fig. S20,S21) by the high-temperature reduction self-assembly. Moreover, these materials also have the superhydrophobic/superoleophilic properties for various applications.
In summary, we have developed novel superhydrophobic/superoleophilic materials of wrinkled microspherical MOF@rGO composites, which possess unique micro/nano-hierarchical architecture consisting of crumpled rGO nanosheets intercalated with well-dispersed MOF nanoparticles. Due to the synergistic effect of superwettability and abundant meso-/micro-porosity, the formed MOF@rGO composites display a higher absorption capacity and selectivity for removal of organic solvents and oils from water than the individual constituents. Further, a ZIF-8@rGO@Sponge adsorbent made from a commercial PU sponge shows prominent oil-water separation performance and good recyclability. Our research work not only provides a promising material for oil-spill cleaning and organic-contaminated wastewater treatment, but also a new concept for the structural and multifunctional exploitation of graphene/MOF-based composite materials.
